\chapter{Resampling}\label{ch:resampling}

Resampling changes the size of an image. It is the operation LineScope was written to study, so
its implementation puts clarity and exactness first. Each menu entry is \emph{exactly} the
textbook algorithm it names. Library routines (vImage, Core Image) choose their own kernels,
prefilters and edge rules, and that would blur the comparison the user is trying to make.

\section{Theory in brief}

A digital image is a set of samples of a continuous picture. To resample it to a new grid we
(conceptually) reconstruct the continuous picture by convolving the samples with a
\emph{kernel} $k$, then sample the result at the new positions. In one dimension, output pixel
$i$ at position $u_i$ in input coordinates is
\[
  \mathrm{out}_i = \frac{\sum_j k(j + \tfrac12 - u_i)\,\mathrm{in}_j}{\sum_j k(j + \tfrac12 - u_i)} .
\]
The denominator renormalizes the weights, so a constant image stays constant even where the
kernel is cut off at the image border.

\paragraph{Pixel centers.} Input pixel $j$ covers the interval $[j, j+1)$ and has its center at
$j + \frac12$. With scale $s = n_{\mathrm{out}}/n_{\mathrm{in}}$, output pixel $i$ covers
$[i, i+1)$ in output coordinates, and its center maps back to
\[
  u_i = \frac{i + \frac12}{s}
\]
in input coordinates. This convention keeps the image centered: halving an image of width 4
puts the two output centers at input positions 1 and 3, exactly between the input centers
0.5, 1.5 and 2.5, 3.5. Using $u_i = i/s$ instead would shift the whole image by half a pixel,
a classic bug that shows up as asymmetric profiles.

\paragraph{Downscaling needs a prefilter.} When $s < 1$ the new grid cannot represent
frequencies above its own Nyquist limit, $s/2$ cycles per input pixel. A reconstruction kernel of
fixed width lets those frequencies through, and they fold back as aliases. The standard cure is
to stretch the kernel by $1/s$, which turns it into a low-pass filter matched to the new grid:
\[
  k_s(x) = k(s\,x), \qquad \text{with support widened by } 1/s .
\]
The normalization above absorbs the factor $s$ that a properly scaled filter would carry.
Nearest neighbor is kept \emph{without} this widening, so that the menu offers a method that
aliases. Seeing aliasing is instructive.

\paragraph{Separability.} All kernels here are applied separably, first along rows, then
along columns: $k(x,y) = k(x)\,k(y)$. That turns an $O(r^2)$ cost per pixel into $O(r)$ and lets both
passes share one routine for computing weights.

